  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "baf726f2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "**Data-quality note first**: an initial pass showed sharp dark-dot artifacts\n",
    "in the position-binned raster. These traced to a real bug in pynapple's\n",
    "`compute_1d_tuning_curves_continuous` -- it computes bin occupancy from the\n",
    "dense ~60Hz position trace but averages the sparse ~30Hz eye-camera samples,\n",
    "so any 2cm bin the animal visited but that happened to contain zero\n",
    "eye-camera frames silently keeps a `0.0` placeholder instead of becoming\n",
    "NaN (~3.8% of bins here). A pixel value of exactly 0 is physically\n",
    "impossible for this data (real range ~90-130), so these were fixed by\n",
    "converting exact-zero bins to NaN after the pynapple call; a separate, much\n",
    "smaller correction (~0.3% of frames) removes genuine blink/tracking-loss\n",
    "frames via the mean DeepLabCut landmark likelihood. Both fixes are applied\n",
    "throughout (including to `eye_dilation`). The first (uncorrected) pass had\n",
    "inflated the apparent anchored-vs-non-anchored gaze difference roughly 5-7x\n",
    "and even flipped its sign -- a reminder that any of this project's future\n",
    "per-session eye-tracking analyses should apply the same exact-zero fix.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Gaze position\n",
    "\n",
    "- **No shift in average gaze location.** Raw per-frame pupil centroid mean\n",
    "  position is essentially identical between anchored and non-anchored\n",
    "  trials (121.8, 95.9) vs (121.8, 96.0) px -- the animal is not looking at a\n",
    "  systematically different place when the population is non-anchored.\n",
    "- **But gaze is modestly less variable/more consistent during anchored\n",
    "  trials**, in two independent metrics that agree in direction:\n",
    "  - *Trial-level*: deviation of each trial's position-referenced gaze\n",
    "    profile from the session's typical (median) profile is smaller on\n",
    "    anchored trials (mean 1.1px) than non-anchored trials (mean 1.2px);\n",
    "    Spearman corr(frac. population anchored, deviation) = -0.17 (p=0.009,\n",
    "    n=225 trials); Mann-Whitney p=0.016.\n",
    "  - *Raw frame-level*: pooled per-frame std is smaller on anchored frames\n",
    "    (1.3, 0.8 px for x, y) than non-anchored frames (1.5, 0.9 px);\n",
    "    Mann-Whitney eye_x p=0.044, eye_y p=1.8e-24.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Pupil size (dilation)\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Pupils are smaller and less variable during anchored trials.**\n",
    "  - *Trial-level*: mean dilation is lower on anchored trials (25.36) than\n",
    "    non-anchored trials (25.59); Spearman corr(frac. population anchored,\n",
    "    trial mean dilation) = -0.26 (p<0.001, n=225 trials); Mann-Whitney\n",
    "    p=0.001.\n",
    "  - *Raw frame-level*: anchored frames mean=24.89, std=1.18; non-anchored\n",
    "    frames mean=25.29, std=1.45 (n=29883 vs 25960 frames); Mann-Whitney\n",
    "    p=1.7e-240 -- the histogram shows a clear rightward-shifted, broader\n",
    "    non-anchored distribution, not just a mean shift.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "Three independent signals -- gaze-profile deviation, raw gaze variability,\n",
    "and pupil dilation -- all point the same direction: population-anchored\n",
    "epochs in M25D24 are associated with a calmer, more stereotyped visual/\n",
    "oculomotor state (smaller, steadier pupils; more repeatable gaze\n",
    "trajectory), while non-anchored epochs show larger, more variable pupils\n",
    "and slightly more gaze drift. This is consistent with a general\n",
    "arousal/engagement account (larger, more variable pupils typically index\n",
    "higher or more labile arousal) co-varying with whether the MEC population\n",
    "is anchored to the task, rather than a simple \"looking at a different\n",
    "screen location\" effect (mean gaze position doesn't shift). Effect sizes\n",
    "are modest throughout (~1-15% relative differences) but statistically\n",
    "robust given the large number of trials/frames, and this is still a single\n",
    "session -- worth checking whether the pattern replicates across the other\n",
    "74 sessions already cached in `all_sessions_rasters/` before treating it as\n",
    "a general finding."
   ]
  }
